@author "Claude (Anthropic)"
@design traditional
@source "[tanpenguin]"

; The traditional penguin, after the seven steps of Kelly Tan's diagram
; [tanpenguin] at https://origami.me/penguin/. The diagram holds the paper
; as a diamond with .a at the bottom and .c at the top, and turns it over
; twice. It asks for 15 cm paper, hence the unit. Where the diagram leaves a
; position to the eye (steps 2, 4 and 7), a free point carries it.

unit cm
paper square 15

; step 1: the vertical diagonal, folded and unfolded with the colored side
; up, then the paper turned over. .h, where step 7 bends the head, is bound
; on it now, a fifth of the way down from the top corner.
mark (through .a .c) (valley) as --mid      ; step 1: fold and unfold
.h = free on --mid from .c at 1/5
flip                                        ; step 1: turn over

; step 2: both top edges in toward the center line, short of it. Each crease
; runs from the top corner to a point of the lower edge 3/10 of the way from
; the side corner to the bottom corner, which leaves the gap the diagram
; shows between the folded edges.
.p = free on --ab from .b at 3/10
.pl = free on --da from .d at 3/10
fold (through .c .p) (moving .b) as --wr    ; step 2, right
fold (through .c .pl) (moving .d) as --wl   ; step 2, left

; The points of steps 4 and 7 on the creases of step 2, bound before step 4
; bends them. --wr is the right edge of the kite in step 4 and the outer
; edge of the body in step 7. Step 4 runs from a fifth of the way up that
; edge from the side corner .p to the middle of the lower edge; step 7
; meets it 2/5 of the way down from the top corner.
.u = free on --wr from .p at 1/5
.ul = free on --wl from .pl at 1/5
.v = free on --ab & .a from .p at 1/2
.vl = free on --da & .a from .pl at 1/2
.e = free on --wr from .c at 2/5

flip                                        ; step 3: turn over

fold (through .u .v) (moving .p) as --cr    ; step 4, right side corner in
fold (through .ul .vl) (moving .pl) as --cl ; step 4, left side corner in

; step 5: the bottom corner up on the line through the lower ends of the
; step 4 creases
fold (through .v .vl) (moving .a) as --s5   ; step 5
.z = --mid * --s5                           ; the bottom of the center line

; step 6: in half on the crease of step 1, the left half onto the right
fold (--mid) (moving .ul) into --mid        ; step 6

; step 7: the top corner outside reverse folded into the head, on the line
; from .h on the folded edge to .e on the outer edge. The diagram draws that
; line about 21 degrees off the perpendicular to the folded edge; .h and .e
; set it at 23. Its drawing of the finished model shows a larger and more
; level head than its line of step 7 gives. Both halves wrap around the
; outside of the body, so .h becomes the top of the head and .c the tip of
; the beak.
reverse (through .h .e) (moving .c) (outside) as --head  ; step 7

; the finished penguin stands on its folded edge, the head up
@orient .a .h up

; assert steps = 7

; Every crease of the program is folded, so no hinge is flat and each face
; is a flap of its own: the flap count is the face count.
; assert faces = 14

; step 5 lands the bottom corner on the center line, and step 6 lays each
; point of the left half on its partner of the right half
; assert .a = (21/4, 21/4)
; assert .ul = .u
; assert .vl = .v
; assert .pl = .p
; assert .d = .b

; the corners of the outline, clockwise from the top of the head .h: the
; back of the head .e, the side corner .u, the bottom corner .v, the bottom
; of the folded edge .z, and the tip of the beak .c
; assert .h = (12, 12)
; assert .e = (66/5, 9)
; assert .u = (57/5, 3)
; assert .v = (21/4, 0)
; assert .z = (21/8, 21/8)
; assert .c = (225/29, 351/29)
